  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>Light Peak</strong> rises to 8,325 feet in Oregon. The Light Peak Trail covers 5.3 miles with about 1,693 feet of elevation gain \u2014 gentle grades, but the mileage is what makes it a Hard outing.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>The climbing is spread fairly evenly from start to finish rather than stacked into one steep section. The steepest stretch arrives around mile 4.55, gaining roughly 800 feet per mile \u2014 expect a slow, deliberate pace through here. From the trailhead at about 6,493 feet, the route tops out near 8,186 feet. You'll cross above treeline on the way up, so the upper route is exposed to weather \u2014 check the forecast and turn around if conditions build.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=42.28856,-120.20268\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">42.28856, -120.20268</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 190 miles north of Reno, Nevada. Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Hard rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 2.5 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Drake Peak, Crane Mountain and Gearhart Mountain \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
